      en: "León, officially known as Santiago de los Caballeros de León, is a historically significant city in northwestern Nicaragua and is regarded as the intellectual heart of the country. Originally founded in 1524 by Francisco Hernández de Córdoba, the city was relocated to its present site in 1610 following a devastating eruption of the Momotombo volcano. For centuries, León served as the liberal capital of Nicaragua, maintaining a fierce rivalry with the conservative stronghold of Granada. The Cathedral of León, a UNESCO World Heritage site, is the largest cathedral in Central America and contains the tomb of the renowned poet Rubén Darío. The city is also home to the country's oldest university, fostering a vibrant student atmosphere and a rich cultural scene. Its architecture is defined by colonial buildings, magnificent churches, and numerous murals that commemorate Nicaragua's revolutionary history. Today, León attracts visitors interested in its colonial heritage and adventure seekers exploring the surrounding volcanic chain.",
